#: src/configuring-zed.md:1
msgid "Configuring Zed"
msgstr ""

#: src/configuring-zed.md:3
msgid ""
"Zed is designed to be configured: we want to fit your workflow and "
"preferences exactly. We provide default settings that are designed to be a "
"comfortable starting point for as many people as possible, but we hope you "
"will enjoy tweaking it to make it feel incredible."
msgstr ""

#: src/configuring-zed.md:5
msgid ""
"In addition to the settings described here, you may also want to change your "
"[theme](./themes.md), configure your [key bindings](./key-bindings.md), set "
"up [tasks](./tasks.md) or install [extensions](https://github.com/zed-"
"industries/extensions)."
msgstr ""

#: src/configuring-zed.md:7
msgid "Settings Editor"
msgstr "設定編輯器"

#: src/configuring-zed.md:9
msgid ""
"You can browse through many of the supported settings via the Settings "
"Editor, which can be opened with the <kbd class=\"keybinding\">cmd-,|ctrl-,</"
"kbd> keybinding, or through the `zed: open settings` action in the command "
"palette. Through it, you can customize your local, user settings as well as "
"project settings."
msgstr ""

#: src/configuring-zed.md:11
msgid ""
"Note that not all settings that Zed supports are available through the "
"Settings Editor yet. Some more intricate ones, such as language formatters, "
"can only be changed through the JSON settings file <kbd "
"class=\"keybinding\">cmd-alt-,|ctrl-alt-,</kbd>."
msgstr ""

#: src/configuring-zed.md:14
msgid "User Settings File"
msgstr "使用者設定檔"

#: src/configuring-zed.md:23
msgid ""
"Your settings JSON file can be opened with <kbd class=\"keybinding\">cmd-"
"alt-,|ctrl-alt-,</kbd>. By default it is located at `~/.config/zed/settings."
"json`, though if you have `XDG_CONFIG_HOME` in your environment on Linux it "
"will be at `$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/zed/settings.json` instead."
msgstr ""

#: src/configuring-zed.md:26
msgid ""
"Whatever you have added to your user settings file gets merged with any "
"local configuration inside your projects."
msgstr ""

#: src/configuring-zed.md:28
msgid "Default Settings"
msgstr "預設設定"

#: src/configuring-zed.md:30
msgid ""
"In the Settings Editor, the values you see set are the default ones. You can "
"also verify them in JSON by running <code class=\"hljs\">zed: open default "
"settings</code> from the command palette."
msgstr ""

#: src/configuring-zed.md:33
msgid ""
"Extensions that provide language servers may also provide default settings "
"for those language servers."
msgstr ""

#: src/configuring-zed.md:35
msgid "Project Settings File"
msgstr "專案設定檔"

#: src/configuring-zed.md:37
msgid ""
"Similarly to user files, you can open your project settings file by running "
"<code class=\"hljs\">zed: open project settings</code> from the command "
"palette. This will create a `.zed` directory containing`.zed/settings.json`."
msgstr ""

#: src/configuring-zed.md:40
msgid ""
"Although most projects will only need one settings file at the root, you can "
"add more local settings files for subdirectories as needed. Not all settings "
"can be set in local files, just those that impact the behavior of the editor "
"and language tooling. For example you can set `tab_size`, `formatter` etc. "
"but not `theme`, `vim_mode` and similar."
msgstr ""

#: src/configuring-zed.md:44
msgid ""
"The syntax for configuration files is a super-set of JSON that allows `//` "
"comments."
msgstr ""

#: src/configuring-zed.md:46
msgid "Per-release Channel Overrides"
msgstr ""

#: src/configuring-zed.md:48
msgid ""
"Zed reads the same `settings.json` across all release channels (Stable, "
"Preview or Nightly). However, you can scope overrides to a specific channel "
"by adding top-level `stable`, `preview`, `nightly` or `dev` objects. They "
"are merged into the base configuration with settings from these keys taking "
"precedence upon launching the specified build. For example:"
msgstr ""

